---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const total = assertion('fifty-nine-thousand-four-hundred-and-thirty-three-buildings')
const industry = assertion('industry-is-a-fortieth-and-a-seventh')
const units = assertion('a-structure-is-not-a-housing-unit')
const cells = assertion('thirty-four-cellblocks-filed-as-houses')
const partition = assertion('thirteen-subdivisions-partition-the-roofs')
const largest = assertion('the-largest-roof-in-the-county')
const coords = assertion('eight-of-ten-coordinates-hit-no-building')
const addresses = assertion('more-buildings-than-addresses')
const religious = assertion('two-hundred-and-fifty-eight-religious-roofs')
const tank = assertion('no-building-stands-where-the-tank-plant-is-mapped')

const inZone = assertion('nine-hundred-and-six-buildings-in-the-floodplain')
const away = assertion('the-county-built-away-from-its-water')
const metals = assertion('thirty-eight-of-a-hundred-metals-buildings')
const manufactured = assertion('a-manufactured-home-is-twice-as-likely')
const people = assertion('thirteen-hundred-and-seventy-six-people')
const leftover = assertion('the-crosswalk-leaves-a-hundred-and-eight-blocks')
const elida = assertion('elida-and-delphos-run-the-other-way')
const villages = assertion('five-villages-with-none')
const claims = assertion('one-claim-for-every-nine-buildings-inside')

const classCats = ['Residential', 'Agriculture', 'Commercial', 'Industrial', 'Government', 'Unclassified', 'Education', 'Assembly', 'Utility and Misc']
const classVals = [48477, 4550, 2455, 1591, 849, 736, 477, 258, 40]

const subCats = ['Lima', 'American', 'Shawnee', 'Bath', 'Marion', 'Richland', 'Perry', 'Spencer', 'Jackson', 'Auglaize', 'Monroe', 'Amanda', 'Sugar Creek']
const subVals = [1141, 306, 247, 178, 103, 91, 90, 87, 74, 68, 55, 53, 48]
const subCount = [15766, 7402, 7242, 5692, 4386, 3829, 2961, 2042, 2672, 2469, 1989, 1831, 1152]

const fzCats = ['Unclassified', 'Education', 'Industrial', 'Government', 'Commercial', 'Residential', 'Agriculture']
const fzVals = [3.67, 3.35, 3.27, 3.18, 2.53, 1.43, 0.66]

const gapCats = ['Delphos', 'Fort Shawnee', 'Lima', 'Lafayette', 'Westminster', 'Bluffton', 'Elida', 'Gomer']
const gapVals = [4.63, -3.46, -3.58, -5.19, -6.16, -11.87, -13.31, -19.27]

const classSpec = bar(classCats, classVals, { yTitle: 'Buildings', labelValues: true })
const subSpec = bar(subCats, subVals, { yTitle: 'Buildings per square mile', labelValues: true })
const fzSpec = bar(fzCats, fzVals, {
  yTitle: 'Per cent of the class inside the flood hazard area',
  labelValues: true,
})
const gapSpec = bar(gapCats, gapVals, {
  yTitle: 'Share of buildings minus share of land, points',
  labelValues: true,
})
---

<Article slug="where-the-buildings-are">
  <p>
    A machine read five aerial photographs of Allen County and drew a polygon around every roof
    bigger than a two-car garage. There are 59,433 of them. Together they cover four thousand two
    hundred acres — one and two thirds per cent of the county — and where they stand settles a
    question the flood map on its own could not: how many people here live on ground that is mapped
    for flooding. The answer is about 1,376, and the last estimate was three times that.
  </p>

  <h2>Fifty-nine thousand roofs</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={total} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="classes"
    title="Four buildings in five are somebody's home"
    caption="Allen County's buildings by the occupancy class an automated classifier assigned. Agriculture is the second largest class and Industrial the fourth, and 736 buildings defeated the classifier entirely."
    columns={['Class', 'Buildings']}
    rows={classCats.map((c, i) => [c, classVals[i].toLocaleString()])}
    height={330}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="classes" set:html={JSON.stringify(classSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={industry} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={units} />
  <Gloss>
    A structure is not a housing unit in either direction. One multi-family roof holds many units,
    and a flat over a shop is filed under Commercial. That the two totals — 48,336 dwelling
    structures and 44,563 housing units — come out within four thousand of each other is two
    opposite errors of roughly the same size, and it should not be read as either file confirming
    the other.
  </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={cells} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={partition} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="subdivisions"
    title="A quarter of the county's buildings on a thirtieth of its ground"
    caption="Buildings per square mile by county subdivision. Lima is denser than the next township by a factor of nearly four, and the twelve townships and the city account for all 59,433 buildings with nothing left over."
    columns={['Subdivision', 'Buildings', 'Per square mile']}
    rows={subCats.map((c, i) => [c, subCount[i].toLocaleString(), subVals[i]])}
    height={330}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="subdivisions" set:html={JSON.stringify(subSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={largest} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={tank} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={coords} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={addresses} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={religious} />

  <h2>Nine hundred and six on the floodplain</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={inZone} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={away} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="floodclass"
    title="Industry, government and schools are on it at twice the rate houses are"
    caption="Per cent of each occupancy class standing inside the special flood hazard area. Farm buildings are the least likely to be on it and the classifier's own residue the most."
    columns={['Class', 'Per cent in the hazard area']}
    rows={fzCats.map((c, i) => [c, fzVals[i]])}
    height={310}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="floodclass" set:html={JSON.stringify(fzSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={metals} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={manufactured} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={people} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={leftover} />
  <Gloss>
    The old figure was 4,448 and it was made by weighting each census block by the fraction of its
    acres inside the hazard boundary. That treats a block's people as spread evenly over its ground,
    which is exactly what settlement is not. Weighting instead by the block's own residential
    buildings — which is where people actually are — gives 1,376. The difference is a factor of
    three and it is not a correction to the flood map; it is a correction to an assumption about
    where houses sit inside a block.
  </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={elida} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="placegap"
    title="Every place but one has fewer buildings on the floodplain than ground"
    caption="Share of a place's buildings inside the hazard area minus the share of its land, in percentage points. Gomer has almost a third of its ground on the floodplain and an eighth of its buildings; Delphos is the only place in the county where the order reverses."
    columns={['Place', 'Difference, points']}
    rows={gapCats.map((c, i) => [c, gapVals[i]])}
    height={310}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="placegap" set:html={JSON.stringify(gapSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={villages} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={claims} />
  <Gloss>
    Among the county's policyholders, the ones rated outside the floodplain produced more claims per
    policy than the ones rated inside — which reads as an argument that the map is drawn in the
    wrong place. Among buildings it reverses: one paid claim for every 8.7 structures inside the
    boundary and one for every 552 outside. Cover is compulsory inside and voluntary outside, so the
    two denominators were recruited by different rules, and the whole distance between those two
    sentences is who chose to buy a policy.
  </Gloss>
</Article>
